INT. SUBWAY CAR - MORNING RUSH

Packed. Elbows. Umbrellas. A tuba case.

Fluorescent lights flicker green. The train rumbles through the dark.

PIP, 10, a girl in a yellow raincoat, clutches a strap too high for her. She hums.

At her feet, a lumpy backpack with a zipper mouth. It wiggles.

ZED (V.O.)
(muffled, from the bag)
Pip. Pip. Are we there?

PIP
(whispering)
Not yet, Zed.

ZED
You said five stops. I counted nine.

Pip's phone buzzes.

ON PHONE: mom: where r u

ON PHONE: mom: ANSWER ME

Pip stares at it. She taps the screen.

ON PHONE: no signal

Her phone. Dead.

Across the aisle, MABEL, 70s, in a purple scarf, knits something long and glittering. She watches Pip over her glasses.

Silence.

The train slows. Brakes squeal.

MABEL
That's a loud bag, dear.

PIP
It's, um. A toy.

MABEL
Toys don't ask how many stops.

The lights go out.

Darkness. Nothing.

Then a soft blue glow. The backpack unzips by itself.

Zed climbs out. A little round robot, one antenna bent.

ZED
Hello, Mabel.

MABEL
Hello, Zed. You're late.

Pip's jaw drops.
